{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And Jethro rejoiced.</b> He rejoiced in the matter, from the language of (I Chronicles 16:27): ״עז וחדוה במקומו״. And further one may say, ויחד is from the language of unity (אחדות), for he converted and declared the unity of HaShem, may He be exalted, and blessed Him, as it says: ״ברוך ה' אשר הציל אתכם״." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> ״ויחד יתרו״ — his flesh became prickles upon prickles (חדודין חדודין). And that is what they say: ״עד עשרה דרי לא תבזי ארמאה באפיה״ — until ten generations, do not despise an Aramaean in his face. And the explanation of עד עשרה דרי is that Jethro was the tenth generation to Mitzraim, son of Ham, and the Egyptians were the men of his covenant and his kinsmen. And moreover, the children of Egypt and the children of Midian were kinsmen, for Midian was the son of Abraham from Keturah, and Abraham descended from Shem, son of Noah, and Mitzraim was the son of Ham, as it says (Genesis 10:6): ״ובני חם כוש ומצרים״. And just as there were ten generations from Noah to Abraham, so too you find ten generations from Ham son of Noah to Midian son of Abraham, and ten generations from Mitzraim son of Ham to Jethro, who was of the children of Midian. Therefore they expounded ״עד עשרה דרי,״ for Jethro, even though he converted and separated himself from them entirely, nevertheless his flesh aches over their punishment even in the tenth generation."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:00:38.787488366Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}